WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:03.060
Now that we've covered the theories and

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.299
principles with how to handle animations within the engine,

3
00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:10.140
we're going to hop right into Unreal and begin animating.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.619
Now that we're back here in the editor,

5
00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:14.019
let's take a look at where to

6
00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:15.859
find what we're going to be working with today.

7
00:00:15.859 --> 00:00:17.899
We've already been inside the animation folder,

8
00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:19.940
but now we're going to go into the mesh.

9
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.620
Let's go ahead and double-click in that.

10
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.940
Then inside, you're going to see

11
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.679
a couple other types of meshes.

12
00:00:25.679 --> 00:00:28.239
You have your skeleton and a skeletal mesh.

13
00:00:28.239 --> 00:00:30.440
Go ahead and double-click on the skeleton.

14
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.450
Here on the left, you're going to see the skeleton tree

15
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.890
set up in a hierarchy, as you can see,

16
00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.549
every bone that is associated with this.

17
00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:42.710
Now, you can select a bone here in this editor.

18
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.909
Now, you will notice you won't be able to do it in here.

19
00:00:44.909 --> 00:00:46.509
So you want to select over here,

20
00:00:46.509 --> 00:00:48.149
we'll see our root bone,

21
00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:50.979
and there's a really cool little shortcut that we can

22
00:00:50.979 --> 00:00:53.579
do to display and use a button.

23
00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:55.579
So I'm going to come over here to Character,

24
00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:57.280
down to Bones.

25
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.920
Now, see any one of these selections,

26
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.359
if you hit Shift,

27
00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:04.400
left mouse button, you're going to create a button.

28
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.680
So I'm going to go ahead and do that for a couple others.

29
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.780
I'm going to do that for selected only,

30
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.239
and let's do another one for none.

31
00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:14.799
So now we have all these buttons, we can select it,

32
00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:17.920
makes it a lot easier in order to show our bones.

33
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.000
Now that we have our skeleton all set and ready to go,

34
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.319
before we add a socket,

35
00:01:22.319 --> 00:01:24.480
we need to understand what a socket is.

36
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.760
So a socket is basically a point that is attached to a bone.

37
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.879
What does this mean?

38
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.560
This means it gives us the ability to attach something

39
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.480
somewhere on our skeleton, say like a razor gun,

40
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.219
or a backpack, or even a hat.

41
00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:38.799
Whatever you decide to do,

42
00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:42.519
what this allows you to do is to swap out said meshes.

43
00:01:42.519 --> 00:01:44.560
So you could change your different types of guns,

44
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.560
different hats, different backpacks.

45
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.379
And this is what this is going to allow us to do.

46
00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:51.219
So let's go ahead and hop back into the engine

47
00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:53.120
and do that.

48
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.099
Here we see our skeletal mesh again,

49
00:01:55.099 --> 00:01:56.879
and we want to attach a socket.

50
00:01:56.879 --> 00:01:58.599
So where do we want to do that?

51
00:01:58.599 --> 00:02:00.500
Well, I'm going to attach a backpack.

52
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.000
So I'm going to select some here along the spine.

53
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.900
I'm going to select the third spine zero three,

54
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:07.980
and then I'm going to come over here

55
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.460
to the skeleton tree, and I'm going to right click.

56
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.240
I'm going to look for this add socket option.

57
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.719
All right.

58
00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:18.560
Now you'll notice it names it as spine zero three socket.

59
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.319
So for referencing later and let's say blueprints,

60
00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:23.439
or even just for general cleanup,

61
00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:27.460
let's go ahead and rename this to backpack.

62
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.699
And you can put SOC or SOC, whatever you want to do.

63
00:02:30.699 --> 00:02:32.520
I'm just do SOC.

64
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:33.360
All right.

65
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.560
Now you'll notice that there's nothing there.

66
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.900
So what do we have to do is we have to add

67
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.259
a preview mesh.

68
00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.620
So let's go ahead and right click on there.

69
00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.180
And you're going to notice this option here

70
00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.539
is add preview.

71
00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:48.340
So let's do a search for a backpack

72
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.539
and we'll see the skeletal mesh of the backpack.

73
00:02:50.539 --> 00:02:52.409
So let's go ahead and click that.

74
00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:54.250
And you'll see, ta-da, it's on there,

75
00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.509
but it looks kind of weird, right?

76
00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:59.389
Well, we have to rotate it in location.

77
00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:00.870
So this is what we're going to do.

78
00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.199
So we're going to kind of move it into place.

79
00:03:04.199 --> 00:03:07.719
I'm going to rotate that about 90 degrees,

80
00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:13.139
about to there and rotate it here, 90 degrees.

81
00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:14.219
That looks good.

82
00:03:14.219 --> 00:03:16.080
And I'm going to move it back to the back

83
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:16.919
to where I like it.

84
00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:18.659
See, this is wherever you want to place it.

85
00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:20.620
So let me make sure it gets pretty close.

86
00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.219
That looks pretty good.

87
00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:23.060
All right.

88
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.979
So now our mesh is in place.

89
00:03:24.979 --> 00:03:26.900
Now you'll notice we didn't rotate it

90
00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:31.620
from the preview socket, but rather the socket itself.

91
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.020
So we didn't do it on the mesh, but on the socket.

92
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:35.819
Now, this is important to note.

93
00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:38.340
If you attach different size objects,

94
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.280
if you scale it or rotate it,

95
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.439
it's going to take on these transforms

96
00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:43.580
that we just applied.

97
00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.439
So just be aware of that when you're building

98
00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:48.539
or adding additional sockets that you want to make sure

99
00:03:48.539 --> 00:03:50.819
that they're rotated in the same direction.

100
00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:53.099
Otherwise you're going to have flipped backpacks

101
00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:55.740
sideways rotated inside the character.

102
00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.800
And that's not what you're going to want to do.

103
00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.020
So that's pretty much it.

104
00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.580
So come up here and hit save.

105
00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.699
And a good habit to get to is to save all,

106
00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:05.620
which is control shift S.

107
00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.289
I like to do that because you never know.

108
00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:09.349
And then you're done.

109
00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.710
So we're going to be able to use this in our animation.

110
00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:13.969
So that's the next thing.

111
00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:16.389
Let's get started on an animation.
